WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.310
Game of Thrones’ Night King isn't a character
known for his friendly face and adorable smile,

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.549
and the extra-weird look he gave Bran Stark
during the Battle of Winterfell had everyone

00:00:09.549 --> 00:00:12.340
wondering what was going on in his frosty
brain.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.140
Here's what was really going on during that
eerie exchange.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.700
You probably remember how the scene went down:
Bran sat beneath the weirwood tree in the

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.360
godswood of Winterfell, awaiting the Night
King's arrival.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:26.820
When the icy villain finally approached, he
ambled toward the youngest living Stark man

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.500
before stopping and staring into his eyes
with an eerie look on his face, an expression

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.920
most fans took as clear intent to kill Bran,
a.k.a the Three-Eyed Raven.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.910
According to the Night King himself, Slovakian
stuntman Vladimír Furdík, that isn't exactly

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.780
what was going on in his own head.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.340
Furdík recently revealed to Vulture the real
reason why the Night King looked at Bran like

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.740
he wanted to turn his insides to icicles.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:55.440
As it turns out, the Night King’s glowering
glare at Bran wasn’t solely motivated by

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:56.572
a desire to kill an enemy.

00:00:56.572 --> 00:01:01.700
In actuality, the strange look he gave Bran
was mostly the result of some interesting

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.220
direction Furdík got ahead of shooting the
scene.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:09.480
The director of the Battle of Winterfell episode,
Miguel Sapochnik, instructed Furdík to walk

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.920
toward Bran Stark actor Isaac Hempstead Wright
and imagine him as a cake that he desperately

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.080
wanted to eat.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:17.780
Kind of weird, right?

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.780
When Furdík breaks it down, though, it’s
actually not the oddest direction in the world,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.800
and he was clear in the interview that he
considers Sapochnik a, quote, “great director,”

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.780
who gave him “very good direction.”

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.280
The actor explained,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.040
“I remember a couple of times when I did
the walk, and I go for my target, which was

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.960
Bran, and he said, 'Bran, he is not man.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.080
He is a cake.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.240
And you would like to eat this cake.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.500
And I would like to see you acting, you're
walking to him, you're walking like you're

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.840
looking for that cake, which is waiting for
you under the tree.'"

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:51.140
Furdik continued to elaborate on the direction,
saying,

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.740
"Adults never run for the cake.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:53.520
They walk.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.260
You can see from an [adult's] body, they're
going for a cake from grandmom, and they're

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.780
looking forward to eating this cake.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.860
When I walk, you can see what Miguel said:
'Don't be a soldier.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:04.360
No.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.920
Just walk, confident.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.360
There's a cake.'"

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.229
"Lemon cake’s my favorite."

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:10.200
"So we’ve been told."

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:14.700
Furdík also shared that he’s still not
sure that the Night King even wanted to kill

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.020
Bran in the first place.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.100
He said to Vulture,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.160
"Look, we think he's going to kill Bran, but
who knows if he's going to kill him?

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.100
He doesn't rush.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.000
Maybe he had different [plans].

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.860
Maybe he wanted to do something else.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:26.380
You know?

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.100
Then [Arya Stark] kills him.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.260
But maybe he was gonna show them, 'No, no,
no, wait, I'm here for something else!'"

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.400
Just as Furdík received instruction on how
the Night King should look at Bran during

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.569
the tense sequence, Hempstead Wright also
spoke at length with director Sapochnik to

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:44.180
decide the way Bran should look back at the
Night King.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.660
The 20-year-old actor revealed to The New
York Times that he and Sapochnik, quote, "came

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.480
up with this idea that the look Bran gives
the Night King is one of pity."

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.460
Hempstead Wright further explained,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.540
"Bran saw the creation of the Night King,
or the first White Walker, or whatever.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:04.010
He realizes that he was once just a normal
guy who was forcibly strapped to a tree, and

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:08.599
had a piece of dragonglass plunged in his
heart...He didn't ask to become this raving,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.200
crazy ice killer…So we tried to get a moment
where Bran is feeling sorry for him.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.360
Bran is looking at this ancient being who
didn't want to become this murderer, but is."

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.920
While Hempstead Wright acknowledged that the
Night King did have it out for the Three-Eyed

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:26.290
Raven, he offered another explanation as to
why he stopped to look at Bran with that haunting

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.260
look on this face:

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.840
"The reason the Night King takes so long is
that he's been programmed to destroy the Three-Eyed

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.760
Raven from the moment he was created, so he's
taking a moment to savor it."

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.860
That pause wound up costing the Night King
everything, as it opened up a window for Arya

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.160
to attack and kill him, plus destroy his pack
of White Walkers and wights in the process.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:52.580
"Just when you think that Jon Snow’s going
to be the hero, again, we realize, that Arya

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.180
appears through the mist."

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.060
In the end, the Night King didn't get to have
his cake or eat it, and the plants that sprouted

00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:02.800
through the snow during the closing moments
of the Game of Thrones series finale seem

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.620
to suggest that he never will.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.040
Winter is over, and the Night King is dead.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.520
Good job, team, you all deserve a snack for
this one.

